{# THE MIDDLE RUNG — the furniture.

   Sidebar, top bar, footer, and one slot in the middle. A page that extends
   THIS rung gets the platform around it and no page frame inside it: a
   full-bleed map, a split view, anything that must reach the edges. Without
   this rung such a page would extend the frame anyway and then fight `.page`
   with negative margins, which is how a layout contract starts being worked
   around instead of used.

   EVERY SOCKET HERE IS THE HOST'S. A module fills none of them, because they
   all need something only an application knows: who is signed in, whether this
   response is an impersonation, what the deployment calls itself. The shell
   asks for none of that and renders honestly without it — a fresh installation
   with no user, no routes and no nav sources still gets a branded, navigable
   shell, and the stand-alone rule is the test of it.

   ON THE data-controller ATTRIBUTES: the shell ships the markup AND the
   behaviour. Markup alone, naming controllers the host is expected to write,
   would leave a fresh installation with a theme toggle that toggles nothing and
   a collapse button that collapses nothing — furniture that looks operable and
   is not. The controllers live in this bundle's own assets/, are
   enabled in the host's assets/controllers.json by Flex on install, and are
   addressed by StimulusBundle's namespaced identifier for a UX package —
   `uhifadhi--shell-bundle--<controller>`. Nothing is built and nothing is
   copied into an application. #}
{% extends '@Shell/document.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <div class="shell">
        {% block shell_sidebar %}
            <aside class="side" id="side" data-controller="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--sidebar">
                <div class="side-top">
                    {% block shell_sidebar_brand %}
                        {%- set _brand = shell_brand() -%}
                        <a class="brand" href="{{ _brand.url }}">
                            {%- include '@Shell/_brand_mark.html.twig' with {size: 32} only -%}
                            <b>{{ _brand.name|upper }}</b>
                        </a>
                    {% endblock %}
                    <button class="collapse-btn" type="button" data-action="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--sidebar#toggle"
                            title="Collapse sidebar" aria-label="Collapse sidebar">{{ ux_icon('shell:chevrons-left') }}</button>
                </div>

                {% block shell_sidebar_nav %}
                    {# The nav CONTRACT. Overriding this block opts out of it entirely —
                       which is supported, and is almost always the wrong answer:
                       the registry is what lets a bundle contribute a row. #}
                    {% include '@Shell/_nav.html.twig' with {sections: shell_nav()} only %}
                {% endblock %}

                {%- set _sidefoot %}{% block shell_sidebar_footer %}{% endblock %}{% endset -%}
                {% if _sidefoot|trim is not empty %}<div class="side-foot">{{ _sidefoot }}</div>{% endif %}
            </aside>
        {% endblock %}

        {% block shell_main %}
            <main class="main">
                {% block shell_banner %}{% endblock %}

                {% block shell_topbar %}
                    <header class="topbar">
                        <span class="tb-right">
                            {# THE SOCKET'S DEFAULT CONTENT IS THE WHOLE TOP-BAR ROW —
                               the alerts bell, the theme toggle, and the viewer's
                               card — not a bare toggle a host bolts things beside. A
                               host that adds its own chrome decides where its
                               additions sit by where it calls parent():

                                 {% block shell_topbar_actions %}
                                     {{ parent() }}
                                     …something of the host's…
                                 {% endblock %}

                               Each default is honest on an installation that has not
                               grown a team yet: the toggle needs no viewer, the bell
                               is a placeholder either way, and the card renders only
                               when a source names someone (below). #}
                            {% block shell_topbar_actions %}
                                {# THE ALERTS BELL — a placeholder, and honestly one.
                                   Alerts is a planned feature with no backend yet
                                   (the nav shows the row "Alerts — planned"), so the
                                   bell renders in the platform's SETTLED treatment for
                                   a thing that is coming: visible, dimmed, not a
                                   control, carrying its own explanation in a title —
                                   the same treatment _nav_item gives a row with
                                   nowhere to go. It carries NO count: a badge with a
                                   number nothing produces is furniture pretending to
                                   work, which is exactly what this shell's wired
                                   controls exist to end. When the alerts backend
                                   lands, the count arrives the way every datum does —
                                   through a contract — and the bell becomes a live
                                   button then, not before. #}
                                <span class="tb-icon off" title="Alerts — planned"
                                      aria-label="Alerts — planned">{{ ux_icon('shell:bell') }}</span>

                                {# THE THEME TOGGLE. Wired to the shell's own
                                   controller, which writes the choice the document's
                                   pre-paint script reads (Theme::CHOICE_KEY). #}
                                <button class="tb-icon" type="button" data-controller="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--theme"
                                        data-action="uhifadhi--shell-bundle--theme#toggle"
                                        title="Toggle theme" aria-label="Toggle theme">{{ ux_icon('shell:sun') }}</button>

                                {# THE VIEWER'S CARD — from the user-badge contract, and
                                   only when it has someone to name. The shell composes
                                   no identity: a host that knows who is signed in fills
                                   the registry with a name, initials and an optional
                                   context line ("UCA · operator"); an installation
                                   with no such source — fresh, a sign-in page, an
                                   anonymous request — renders no card rather than an
                                   empty one. #}
                                {%- set _badge = shell_user_badge() -%}
                                {%- if _badge is not null -%}
                                    <span class="user">
                                        <span class="avatar">{{ _badge.initials }}</span>
                                        <span class="uinfo"><b>{{ _badge.name }}</b>
                                            {%- if _badge.context is not null %}<em>{{ _badge.context }}</em>{% endif -%}
                                        </span>
                                    </span>
                                {%- endif -%}
                            {% endblock %}
                        </span>
                    </header>
                {% endblock %}

                {# THE IMPERSONATION BAND — on every page while an administrator
                   has borrowed a session, under the top bar and sticky with it:
                   whose session this is, whose it really is, and the one way
                   out. It exists only on a switched token; nobody else ever
                   sees a trace of it. #}
                {% block shell_impersonation %}
                    {%- set _imp = shell_impersonation() -%}
                    {%- if _imp -%}
                        {%- set _home = shell_brand().url -%}
                        <div class="impband" role="status">
                            {{ ux_icon('shell:eye', {width: 15, height: 15}) }}
                            <span class="who">Signed in as <b>{{ _imp.borrowed }}</b> <em>&middot; you are {{ _imp.real }}</em></span>
                            <a class="impx" href="{{ _home }}?_switch_user=_exit">{{ ux_icon('shell:power', {width: 13, height: 13}) }} Exit</a>
                        </div>
                    {%- endif -%}
                {% endblock %}

                {# THE COMPATIBILITY SOCKET, named for compatibility rather than for
                   clarity and kept on purpose: every host page and every module
                   bundle already fills it. Renaming it would break five
                   repositories in one commit — exactly the casual rewiring this
                   bundle exists to end. The page frame fills it with a framed page;
                   a full-bleed screen fills it directly. #}
                {% block content %}{% endblock %}

                {# NOT `.page`: the frame owns that class and owns exactly one of
                   them per screen, so the footer row carries its own. #}
                {%- set _foot %}{% block shell_footer %}{% endblock %}{% endset -%}
                {% if _foot|trim is not empty %}<div class="shell-foot"><div class="footer">{{ _foot }}</div></div>{% endif %}
            </main>
        {% endblock %}
    </div>
{% endblock %}
